# The canon: fixed frames that say what the game looks like. tools/canon.sh shoots
# them (seed 7) and lays them beside the accepted set on one contact sheet.
#
# Every frame here is pinned. The canon is a REFERENCE: two runs at the same
# commit must differ only where the game changed, so nothing in it may be left
# to what the weather rules roll at the minute the frame happens to be taken.
# `weather clear:0` holds for the rest of the run (Weather.force), and the two
# frames that are about rain ask for rain and then give the clear sky back.
# (Playtest wave A finding 9: the snowfield frames moved 4.14 between two runs
# at one commit, against a tolerance of 3, because its climate rolls whiteouts
# and a whiteout over white ground moves a great many pixels.)
#
# THE SECOND THING THAT MOVED, and it was not the weather: LIVE WORLD STATE.
# Two runs at one commit came out 4.00 apart on 13-spawn-night and 5.99 on
# 16-burning-dusk, mean 1.69 over the eighteen. It was nearly reported as a
# rendering change somebody else had made. It was none of the obvious suspects
# and each was ruled out by measurement rather than by argument:
#   the CAMERA had not moved   -- aligning the two runs over a +/-40px search
#                                 found no shift that improved them, 0 in x and y
#   CAPTURE was deterministic  -- two identical tools/shot.sh runs of one frame
#                                 differ by 0.227, signed luma +0.003
#   ELAPSED TIME was not it    -- the same moment caught after 8, 60 and 120
#                                 frames moves luma by 0.34 in total
# Drawing the difference map showed what it really was: the sea's wave and surf
# phase, the villagers, the player's own animation and the HUD message line. A
# tour's `wait` is a REAL-TIME timer while the game simulates in
# _physics_process, so a busy machine puts a different amount of world in front
# of each shutter. 09-eco-pinewood-snowfield is the control -- no sea, signed
# delta +0.00, and what little it moved was tree sway.
#
# So the canon runs in SIMULATED time (tools/canon.sh sets TOUR_FIXED_FPS), which
# took those two frames to 0.13 and 0.47 and the mean to 0.63 across runs at load
# 31 and load 40. And the HOUR is pinned below, because frames 01-11 used to run
# at "the start hour plus however long boot happened to take": the moss declares
# the deepest dawn mist in the game (BiomeDef.mist 1.0) and Weather.mist is a
# function of minutes, so 03-moss alone still drifted 2.31 of its 3 allowance on
# the clock. Anything added here that is about a MOMENT must pin its hour too.
weather clear:0
hour 8
place spawn
wait 0.8
shot 01-spawn-morning
place coast
wait 0.8
shot 02-coast with land:coast
place moss
wait 0.8
shot 03-moss with land:moss
place pinewood
wait 0.8
shot 04-pinewood with land:pinewood
place snowfield
wait 0.8
shot 05-snowfield with land:snowfield
place bonelands
wait 0.8
shot 06-bonelands with land:bonelands
place burning
wait 0.8
shot 07-burning with land:burning
place coast-pinewood
wait 0.8
shot 08-eco-coast-pinewood with border:coast-pinewood
place pinewood-snowfield
wait 0.8
shot 09-eco-pinewood-snowfield with border:pinewood-snowfield
place bonelands-burning
wait 0.8
shot 10-eco-bonelands-burning with border:bonelands-burning
place coast-moss
wait 0.8
shot 11-eco-coast-moss with border:coast-moss
place spawn
wait 0.8
hour 19.5
wait 0.4
shot 12-spawn-dusk
hour 23
wait 0.4
shot 13-spawn-night
tap lamp
wait 0.4
shot 14-night-lamp with lamp
hour 12
place snowfield
wait 0.8
wait 0.3
shot 15-snowfield-noon with land:snowfield
place burning
wait 0.8
hour 20
wait 0.3
shot 16-burning-dusk with land:burning

# The two things the canon never showed: wet ground mirroring light, and the
# stolen neon somebody wired into a house. The neon frame goes to the landmark
# where a shack wired the machines' light into its wall (GenWorks records it, and
# `place` fails if the world has none), and then asks the picture itself whether
# a tube is in it: the frame used to be shot at the spawn village, where the
# canon seed has no neon shack at all, so the reference for "stolen neon" held
# none of the three tube colours and could not have caught a regression in them.
place spawn
wait 0.8
hour 22
# Thin rain here too, for the reason frame 18 gives: at rain:1 the streaks
# alone walked two runs 1.9-2.25 apart against the sheet's tolerance of 3.
weather rain:0.45
wait 1.2
shot 17-village-wet-night
# Thin rain, not a downpour: the ground still mirrors the tube, and the frame
# stops moving. At rain:1 and this zoom the streaks alone walked two runs 2.9
# apart, against the sheet's tolerance of 3 -- a reference frame one bad roll
# from crying regression.
place stolen_light
weather rain:0.45
zoom 9
wait 1.2
walk 0,1 1.2
wait 0.6
# A tenth of what a real run holds: measured 1628 pixels of stolen neon in
# this frame, against a 60 that would have passed on a speck of it.
shot 18-stolen-neon-close with pixels:4df2ff+ff40cc+8cff59:160
zoom 14
weather clear:0
